WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320
If you're bringing a React Native project into a standard web CICD pipeline, you'll hit a wall quickly.

2
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.400
Moving a project to mobile requires a shift away from the single-threaded build process common in web development.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.520
On the web, your build pipeline usually terminates here, assembling a JavaScript bundle.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.320
Once generated, the compilation is mostly done.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.560
React Native requires that exact bundle, but routes it into a secondary parallel track for native compilation.

6
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.280
That second track takes the bundled code and wraps it into a standalone native executable,

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.240
an Android package. To set this up, you'll need a React Native project hosted on GitHub,

8
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.080
a basic understanding of YAML configuration, and pre-configured scripts for

9
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.640
linting and testing your code. Our objective is to configure a workflow that automatically

10
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.240
delivers a tangible, installable Android APK every single time you push code to the repository.

11
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.600
We'll start by initializing a standard GitHub actions YAML file.

12
00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:01.280
First, we check out the code. Next, we set up node. Then we install dependencies.

13
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.280
We run our code limter and execute our automated tests.

14
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.680
Those steps handle the website of the equation. Now we have to satisfy the mobile requirement.

15
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.160
To compile the actual application, we invoke Gradle, the essential build tool for Android

16
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.440
environments. A common mistake here is trying to run the build tool from the root directory.

17
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.080
You must instruct the runner to change directories into the Android folder first.

18
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.800
Once inside, you execute slash slash Gradle, assemble release to compile the production build.

19
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.560
Unlike those earlier web steps that yield static files, successfully executing this Gradle

20
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.720
command compiles a standalone operating system executable. At this point, the build is complete,

21
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.800
but this entire process happened inside an ephemeral cloud virtual machine. Without an artifact upload

22
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.000
step, the moment the workflow finishes, that virtual machine shuts down, and your freshly

23
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.200
compiled APK is instantly destroyed. To solve this, we add the actions slash upload artifact step.

24
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.920
We point it to the exact directory where Gradle left the file, targeting any file ending in .apk.

25
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.520
This step reaches into the virtual machine before it shuts down, grabs the APK,

26
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.360
and routes it directly to your GitHub dashboard. Automating this capture ensures that every successful

27
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.920
run produces a testable file that persists beyond the temporary life of the runner.

28
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.800
Eventually, a build will fail, and you'll be greeted by the GitHub actions error log.

29
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.560
At first glance, this log is intimidating. It's packed with irrelevant JavaScript bundle noise

30
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.960
and generic Java warnings that obscure the actual issue. To navigate this, bypass all the JS steps

31
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.480
entirely and locate the specific section where the Gradle execution failed. You are looking for a

32
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:59.280
specific keyword string, task, colon, app, colon, assemble, release, failed. Once you spot that exact

33
00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.520
line, look directly at the three to five lines immediately preceding it. That is where you will find

34
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.360
the actual human readable error. Learning to filter this noise focuses your troubleshooting on the

35
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.520
specific line of code or configuration that actually broke the build. Now, let's look at a pipeline

36
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.360
run that successfully completed the entire process. If you scroll to the bottom of the run summary,

37
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.120
you'll locate the downloadable artifact. Automated artifact generation immediately empowers

38
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.040
quality assurance teams with testable software without requiring manual compilation on local machines.

39
00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.880
You can download that file right now and drag it directly onto an Android emulator to verify

40
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:47.440
the changes. Transitioning from web to react native CI requires mastering native build tools,

41
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:57.920
turning simple code pushes into immediately testable mobile applications.

